When you call `this.internalMethod()` inside a Spring bean, `this` refers to the **original object**, not the proxy. The proxy sits between the caller and the bean — it only intercepts calls that arrive through the injected reference.

**The most common AOP bug**
- A public method calls a private method annotated with `@Transactional` — the transaction never starts because the call never goes through the proxy
- Same applies to `@Cacheable`, `@Async`, `@Retry`, custom aspects

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        validate(order);
        this.saveOrder(order);  // bypasses proxy — no transaction!
    }

    @Transactional
    public void saveOrder(Order order) {
        repository.save(order);
    }
}
```

**Three fixes with different trade-offs**
- **Restructure** — move `saveOrder()` to a separate bean so the call crosses the proxy boundary; cleanest solution
- **Self-injection** — inject the bean into itself (`@Autowired private OrderService self`) and call `self.saveOrder()`; works because `self` is the proxy reference
- **AspectJ weaving** — switch from Spring AOP to compile-time AspectJ; modifies bytecode directly, doesn't rely on proxies; the nuclear option, use only if you genuinely need self-invocation aspects

**Two related CGLIB gotchas**
- CGLIB **cannot proxy `final` classes or `final` methods** — it creates a subclass; a `@Cacheable` annotation on a `final` method silently does nothing; Spring logs a warning but doesn't fail the application
- CGLIB **calls the target class's no-arg constructor** to create the proxy subclass — any constructor side effect (logging, registration, initialization) runs twice; harmless for idempotent constructors, latent bug for stateful ones

The `execution()` designator matches based on method signatures. Its syntax looks complex but follows a fixed pattern.

**Syntax**
- `execution(modifiers? return-type declaring-type?.method-name(params) throws?)`
- `*` matches one segment
- `..` matches zero or more (sub-packages, parameters)

**Common patterns**
```java
// All public methods in the service package
@Before("execution(public * com.example.service.*.*(..))")

// All methods returning void in any subpackage
@Before("execution(void com.example..*.*(..))")

// All methods named find* with any parameters
@Before("execution(* com.example..find*(..))")

// Methods taking exactly one String parameter
@Before("execution(* *..*(String))")

// Methods with no parameters
@Before("execution(* *..*())")
```

**The dot-dot trap**
- `..` in `com.example..` matches any number of sub-packages
- `..` in `(..)` matches any number of parameters of any type
- Without `..` for packages, only the exact package is matched — common mistake when methods live in sub-packages

**Performance — avoid overly broad patterns**
- `execution(* *.*(..))` causes Spring to proxy every bean and intercept every method call
- For a large application, this adds measurable latency to every operation
- Scope your patterns to specific packages or use annotation-based pointcuts for precision

The `@annotation()` designator is the safest approach for custom aspects because it's explicitly opt-in: only methods (or classes) carrying a specific annotation are matched.

**Annotation matching patterns**
```java
// Match any method annotated with @Timed
@Around("@annotation(com.example.annotation.Timed)")

// Match any method in a class annotated with @Audited
@Before("@within(com.example.annotation.Audited)")

// Bind the annotation value to an advice parameter
@Around("@annotation(timed)")
public Object time(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp, Timed timed) {
    String metricName = timed.value();
}
```

**Lowercase name binds the annotation instance**
- `@annotation(timed)` (lowercase) binds the annotation to a parameter named `timed`
- Gives you access to annotation attributes inside the advice
- Useful for configurable aspects where the annotation carries metadata

**Combining pointcuts with `&&`, `||`, `!`**
```java
@Pointcut("execution(* com.example.service..*.*(..))")
public void serviceLayer() {}

@Pointcut("@annotation(Transactional)")
public void transactional() {}

@Before("serviceLayer() && !transactional()")
public void logNonTransactional(JoinPoint jp) {
    // Fires on service methods that are NOT @Transactional
}
```

**Reusable pointcut definitions**
- Extract reusable pointcuts as `@Pointcut`-annotated void methods
- Avoids duplicating the same expression across multiple advice methods
- Gives the pointcut a descriptive name
- When the package structure changes, you update one `@Pointcut` method instead of hunting through every advice annotation
